1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:04,721
The brush clears a stone gateway, carved with panels.

2
00:00:04,721 --> 00:00:10,992
It stands at a stupa in central India, but it was paid for by the Satavahanas, who ruled the Deccan.

3
00:00:10,992 --> 00:00:16,735
Their kings took names from their mothers. Why did a king take his mother's name?

4
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:41,677
How do we know? The Nashik inscription praises King Gautamiputra. Lead coins carry the names of kings.

5
00:00:41,677 --> 00:00:50,248
The Puranas call the dynasty the Andhras. A book of Prakrit verses, the Gathasaptashati, is credited to King Hala.

6
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:13,856
The Satavahanas rose in the Deccan about the first century BCE, after the Mauryas. Simuka is named as the founder.

7
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:22,836
Their capital was Pratishthana, now Paithan. Their greatest king was Gautamiputra Satakarni, about 106 to 130 CE.

8
00:01:22,836 --> 00:01:29,733
His mother, Gautami Balashri, left the Nashik inscription, which says he destroyed the Shakas and Pahlavas.

9
00:01:29,733 --> 00:01:36,020
Satavahana kings used their mother's name before their own, like Gautamiputra, son of Gautami.

10
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:43,711
This shows that royal women were honoured. They gave land grants, free of tax, to Brahmins and Buddhist monks.

11
00:01:43,711 --> 00:01:49,051
This was the start of a long tradition. The Satavahanas were great patrons of art.

12
00:01:49,051 --> 00:01:55,117
The stupa gateways at Sanchi, the caves at Karle and the stupa at Amaravati belong to this age.

13
00:01:55,117 --> 00:02:02,808
Their coins were of lead, and their trade reached Rome through ports on the west coast. Prakrit was their language.

14
00:02:18,517 --> 00:02:25,064
Old name, new name. The Puranas call this dynasty the Andhras, after the region of Andhra.

15
00:02:25,064 --> 00:02:34,168
Inscriptions and coins call them the Satavahanas. Historians use the second name, since it is the one the rulers used themselves.

16
00:02:49,883 --> 00:02:57,460
Carve this tip into memory. Satavahana: mother-names. tax-free grants, torana gates.

17
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:03,641
Remember the mother-names and the free land grants. Both are favourite exam points.

18
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:35,373
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name this carved gateway of a stupa. The answer: a torana. Number two.

19
00:03:35,373 --> 00:03:47,559
Which inscription records Gautamiputra’s victories? The answer: the Nashik inscription. Number three.

20
00:03:47,559 --> 00:03:59,207
Of what metal were most Satavahana coins made? The answer: lead. Now one line for Mains.

21
00:03:59,207 --> 00:04:03,861
Discuss the importance of the Satavahanas in the history of the Deccan.

22
00:04:23,583 --> 00:04:31,066
The gateway goes into its case. It is carved with scenes from the life of a faith. Three facts to take away.

23
00:04:31,066 --> 00:04:42,168
A torana, or stupa gateway. Funded in the Satavahana age. Kings used their mothers’ names. Next gallery: The Sangam Age.

